# Copy to .dev.vars for local `npm run dev`
#
# Optional keys are commented out until you need them: uncomment the line and
# put the value after the `=`. A value on a line that still starts with `#` is
# ignored — by the Worker, and by `npm run secrets:put`, which then reports the
# key as having no local value.
#
# Required, and the only secret a deployment has to bring. Generate one with
# `openssl rand -hex 32` — it encrypts the provider tokens stored in D1, and
# AUTH_SECRET and SCHEDULER_SECRET are derived from it (see below). It cannot be
# generated at deploy time: a copy stored next to the data it protects would
# make a database leak a full takeover. `npm run setup` generates it for you when
# it is empty.
# Example (never deploy it): deadbeefdeadbeefdeadbeefdeadbeefdeadbeefdeadbeefdeadbeefdeadbeef
APP_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Optional. A deployment cannot know its own URL before it exists, so the app
# uses the origin of the request it is serving; set this only to pin a custom
# domain or a deliberate public origin. Local dev needs nothing here — the
# derived origin is http://localhost:5173. Opening a dev server from another
# device (a LAN address) does not count as local: generate real keys for that.
# APP_URL=http://localhost:5173

# Optional. The instance name is set in the app (Settings → Instance) and falls
# back to "CogSend"; the var still works for config-file deployments.
# APP_NAME=CogSend

# The login is not configured here. `npm run setup` creates the single account in
# D1 (only a PBKDF2 hash is stored) and it is changed in Settings → Login or
# replaced with `npm run admin:reset`. For local development a row for this
# database comes from `npm run db:seed:local`, and the TOTP prompt is skipped
# with SKIP_TOTP below.

# Both derived from APP_ENCRYPTION_KEY when unset, so leave them empty unless
# you want them independent. AUTH_SECRET signs sessions (changing it signs
# everyone out); SCHEDULER_SECRET is the tick bearer, needed here only when
# something outside the Worker — an external pinger — has to hold it.
# AUTH_SECRET=
# SCHEDULER_SECRET=

# Optional. Scripts send: Authorization: Bearer $API_TOKEN (min 16 chars)
# API_TOKEN=
# Optional. Enables the LinkedIn / Threads / X connect buttons (OAuth apps).
# The accounts dialog can store these instead; a value set here wins.
# LINKEDIN_CLIENT_ID=
# LINKEDIN_CLIENT_SECRET=
# THREADS_APP_ID=
# THREADS_APP_SECRET=
# X_CLIENT_ID=
# X_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Optional. Local dev only: skip the 2FA enrollment/verify flow. Honored only
# when the instance resolves to a localhost URL — never on a real host.
# Set to 1 to enable.
# SKIP_TOTP=1
# Optional. Enables video uploads (LinkedIn only: one MP4 per post, no images
# mixed in). The upload path is wired but has not been verified end to end
# against LinkedIn's live API, so it is off unless you set this. The file
# picker hides video while it is off, and the API refuses video files.
# ENABLE_VIDEO_UPLOAD=1
# Optional. Calls one request may make (D1 statements, R2 operations, platform
# requests). Unset means the Workers Free plan's 50, and the scheduler publishes
# what fits and leaves the rest for the next tick. On a paid Workers plan, set
# 10000 to publish everything due at once.
# SUBREQUEST_LIMIT=10000

# Optional. Failure digest via Resend: the API key plus the address that
# should receive it. Unset means failures only show in the app.
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# NOTIFY_EMAIL=
# NOTIFY_FROM=CogSend <onboarding@resend.dev>
# Optional. Public media origin for Meta's crawler (for example an R2 custom
# domain with Cloudflare cache). When set, Threads gets direct object URLs
# instead of the signed Worker route. Must be https and serve the bucket keys.
# This trades the signature for speed: those URLs never expire and rely only on
# the randomness in the object key, so keep the origin unlisted and treat a
# leaked URL as permanent. Unset it to go back to short-lived signed URLs.
# MEDIA_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://media.example.com
